  ],
  "text": "Cash machines were supposed to end bank tellers. The number of tellers went up for decades afterwards. That is not a happy ending. It is a warning against the obvious prediction. The machine made a branch cheaper to run, so the banks opened many more branches. And the teller's job shifted from counting cash to selling and advising. The pattern is that tasks get automated long before whole jobs do. Spreadsheets did the same. Bookkeeping clerks fell. Accountants and analysts rose. But be honest about what is different. The speed of adoption is unlike anything before it. And it lands on cognitive work, which is the part nobody had planned for. Aggregate numbers can look fine while the disruption is total for the people it lands on. Both the doom and the hype dodge the actual question, which is who absorbs the cost. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
